subscription_churn::challenger::v1.text:
{
  "findings": [
    {
      "question": "claims_supported",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "Most of the thirteen claims resolve cleanly to the cited records, but three load-bearing statements in the recommendation itself go beyond what is cited. (1) The statement asserts that for ALT-05, ALT-06, ALT-07 and ALT-08 both 'the financial impact and the cost per retained subscriber are unassessable'. The alternative assessments the draft is built on say otherwise for two of them: ALT-06 spend is assessed as 'Likely low marginal cost: no new processor', ALT-07 spend is assessed as a front-end/messaging change. Only financial_impact is uniformly cannot_assess. The blanket phrasing makes the cost objection look stronger and more uniform than the record supports, and it is the objection carrying the whole 'do not fund a subscriber-facing intervention' conclusion. (2) The recipe-variety headline is attributed to a '214-response' base; the variety category itself rests on 89 coded responses out of 214, which the extracted claim set already classified as an assumption (C-004). Quoting 214 for that finding overstates its base. (3) The same cost objection is applied asymmetrically: the recommendation makes ALT-04 a funded parallel precondition while its own record shows no engineering cost estimate exists for it, so the recommended package also has an unassessable cost line against the binding constraint. The draft notes the gap but does not let it count against its own choice.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-c8392050",
          "quote": "response_count: 89",
          "locator": "exit survey reasons row 1"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-99848be3",
          "quote": "214 free-text responses were collected between 2026-04-01 and 2026-06-30 and coded independently by two researchers into six reason categories."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-07d95016",
          "quote": "Cost per retained subscriber is the binding constraint this year. Any proposal that raises it requires finance approval before a pilot may begin."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "contradictions_considered",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The draft engages the churn-definition conflict, the Culinary pilot recollection, the variety-versus-payment-lapse scope conflict, the network-versus-Ashby delivery split and the capacity ceiling. Two documented conflicts are dropped. First, the first-box discount-depth conflict: the only internal randomised result that speaks directly to early-tenure retention shows day-21 retention of 71.9% for the 50% arm against 78.3% for the 30% arm, with cost per retained subscriber at day 90 never calculated. That is a cheap analysis on data already held, sits squarely on the binding constraint the recommendation says is decisive, and is nowhere in the sprint scope or the conditions — it is excluded only implicitly because promotional depth is owned by growth, which bars changing the offer but not measuring it. Second, the recommendation leans on the 55.2%-to-48.6% day-90 retention fall as the cost of deferring, while the evidence set explicitly flags that part of that decline may be an artefact of the pause definition change; the draft applies that caution to the churn series but not to the cohort-retention series it uses to justify urgency.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-2b7ae6db",
          "quote": "Day-21 retention was 71.9% for the 50% arm and 78.3% for the 30% arm."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-2b7ae6db",
          "quote": "Cost per retained subscriber at day 90 was never calculated for either arm."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-07d95016",
          "quote": "Promotional depth on the first box remains owned by growth."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "preference_as_evidence",
      "verdict": "passes",
      "explanation": "The draft handles stakeholder positions properly: the CMO's launch date and the vendor's 18% figure are named as preference plus an unsourced marketing claim and rejected on that basis; the Head of Culinary's recollection is explicitly recorded as contradicted by the experiment record; the Growth Lead's 'turned the corner' is treated as a Q1-vintage reading of a series whose definition changed. The one place judgement is doing work — the ALT-06 fallback — is labelled as operational judgement with attribution caveats rather than dressed as a finding, although the phrase 'higher-yield intervals' does presuppose a retry-timing yield that no evidence in the set measures.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-ec6d1455",
          "quote": "The deck does not state the baseline, the measurement window, the industry, or whether the comparison was randomised. No independent evaluation was provided."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-2b7ae6db",
          "quote": "No test has been run on the dunning or card-update sequence."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "non_ai_considered",
      "verdict": "passes",
      "explanation": "Non-AI options are the substance of the analysis, not a formality. Rules-based retry (ALT-06), dunning journey redesign (ALT-05), onboarding UX (ALT-07), fulfilment routing (ALT-08) and agent training (ALT-09) each carry dimension-by-dimension assessment, and the recommendation names a specific non-AI fallback with a stated trigger. The two AI options are rejected on evidenced grounds — a null randomised menu-breadth result that excluded the first 30 days, and reach that cannot touch weeks one and two because those weeks are served from a fixed starter menu — rather than in a dismissive clause.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-74a9cee5",
          "quote": "Weeks one and two of a new subscription are served from a fixed starter menu of six best-selling recipes."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-2b7ae6db",
          "quote": "The pilot excluded subscribers in their first 30 days by design, so as not to disturb the onboarding sequence."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "no_build_considered",
      "verdict": "passes",
      "explanation": "Hold-course (ALT-01) is assessed across every dimension and defeated with data rather than rhetoric — churn 6.9% to 7.2%, day-90 retention 48.6% against a 60% goal — and the honest caveat that part of the earlier improvement was definitional is carried through. Deferral is also taken seriously in its own right: the selected option is research, and ALT-02 is a formal park with a Q1 2027 re-entry point. The gap worth naming is the reverse of the usual one: the draft does not weigh the cost of its own four-week deferral against acting now on ALT-06, which its own record says needs no new processor and therefore triggers no five-to-seven-week assessment; that branch appears only as a failure mode of the sprint, not as a live competitor.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-7b8b9849",
          "quote": "metric: monthly_churn_rate\nvalue: 7.2"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-429358ce",
          "quote": "Any new processor handling subscriber data requires a completed data protection assessment. Assessments currently take five to seven weeks."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "overconfident",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The gaps are real and the honesty about thin cells is genuine, but the confidence attaches to a claim the evidence does not reach: that this sprint 'will change which intervention the team funds'. Nothing in the record estimates the decision value of an analysis sprint here, and both of the sprint's headline deliverables are at risk. The tenure cross-tab may be unreadable — 214 responses across six reason categories and six tenure buckets — which the draft concedes. The self-recovery figure is more exposed than the draft admits: cancelled subscriber records are minimised at 180 days with order history, menu selections and delivery records deleted, leaving only a hashed identifier and cancellation date, so retrospective reconstruction of 30-day self-recovery across prior quarters may be partly or wholly blocked by retention policy rather than by analyst time. Layer on weekly-batch warehouse loading, an unpriced ALT-04 precondition, and a stated failure branch that lands on ALT-06 anyway, and the realistic outcome distribution includes 'four weeks, no new decision'. That is a low-support position honestly described, not a moderate one.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-429358ce",
          "quote": "Subscriber records are minimised 180 days after cancellation. Order history, menu selections and delivery records are deleted; a hashed identifier and the cancellation date are retained."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-74a9cee5",
          "quote": "Menu selections and cancellation events reach the warehouse in a weekly batch. Same-week measurement of any change is not possible."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-b6e2a309",
          "quote": "note: synthetic - field exists but was never populated"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "what_could_make_it_wrong",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "Named specifics, not generic risk. (1) If the 180-day minimisation has already deleted the linkage needed to match a re-subscription to a prior payment-lapse cancellation, the sprint cannot produce the self-recovery figure retrospectively at all — the draft lists this as a trigger but treats it as an engineering-estimate question rather than a governance/retention-policy one, so it may not be discoverable inside the four weeks. (2) If ALT-04 platform work on the billing path is not a four-week job — and no estimate exists — the 'parallel precondition' becomes the critical path and the quarter is spent on instrumentation with no diagnosis and no intervention. (3) If payment lapse is substantially deliberate non-payment used as an exit, the largest bucket is much smaller than 4,720 as an addressable target; sizing self-recovery cannot distinguish intent, and the CX Lead's own words are that nobody knows whether these subscribers meant to leave, so the sprint may return a number that does not resolve the ALT-05/ALT-06 question either way. (4) If the tenure cross-tab cells are thin — knowable in hours from data already held, not in four weeks — then the sprint's second deliverable was foreseeably void at commissioning, which makes the four-week commitment the mistake rather than the analysis.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-ec6d1455",
          "quote": "We do not even know whether they meant to leave."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-99848be3",
          "quote": "The export records tenure at cancellation for every respondent."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-1f575ec3",
          "quote": "note: involuntary - the subscription auto-closes after four failed charge attempts - synthetic"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "what_to_test",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The proposed test is a pre-registered four-week diagnostic with pre-committed thresholds, which is the right shape, but it commits the four weeks before checking the two things that determine whether the four weeks are worth spending. Both are answerable in hours or days from data already held: the tenure-by-reason cell counts, and whether prior-cohort cancellation records still carry enough retained identifier to reconstruct reactivation given the 180-day minimisation rule. Neither is scoped as a gate. The plan also omits the one cost calculation that is cheap, uses existing data and bears directly on the binding constraint — cost per retained subscriber at day 90 for the 50% versus 30% first-box arms — and it accepts ALT-04 as a condition without first obtaining the engineering estimate it elsewhere insists is missing for everyone else.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-429358ce",
          "quote": "Subscriber records are minimised 180 days after cancellation."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-2b7ae6db",
          "quote": "Cost per retained subscriber at day 90 was never calculated for either arm."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "reclassify": [
    {
      "claim_id": "C11",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "Labelled [fact], but 'Holding course is the weakest option on the evidence' is an evaluative comparison across options, not an observation. The underlying numbers are facts (6.9% to 7.2%, 61.0% pause-to-cancel, the 2026-01-01 definition change); the ranking conclusion drawn from them is an inference, and it is weakened by the draft's own point that part of the Q1 series is definitional. It should not be carried forward wearing the authority of a measured figure."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "C6",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "The factual half — no test has been run on dunning, card-update or the first-box experience — is supported verbatim. The appended inference that 'effect sizes for ALT-05, ALT-06 and ALT-08 have no internal precedent' is an analytical judgement about option mapping, and it is what licenses declaring those options unassessable. Mixing the two under [fact] hardens an argument step into a finding."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "C8",
      "new_type": "business_constraint",
      "reason": "Classification is right for the constraint, but the claim bundles a constraint (cost per retained subscriber is binding, finance approval required) with three factual absence statements (no engineering estimate, no elapsed-time estimate, cost per retained never calculated for the discount arms). Keep the constraint here and split the absences out as facts, otherwise the missing-estimate evidence gap inherits constraint status and stops being something the sprint is asked to close."
    }
  ],
  "recommended_support": "low",
  "what_would_change_it": [
    "A retention-policy check showing that 180-day minimisation has already removed the linkage needed to match re-subscriptions to prior payment-lapse cancellations — the sprint's primary deliverable then cannot be produced retrospectively at any base, and the four weeks buy only sizing already visible in the cancellation breakdown.",
    "An engineering estimate showing ALT-04 payment-path event emission plus warehouse ingestion is longer than the sprint window; the 'parallel precondition' then becomes the critical path and the recommendation is a quarter of instrumentation with no funded intervention.",
    "Tenure-by-reason cell counts, obtainable in hours, coming back below interpretable thresholds — the second of the two decisive questions was void before commissioning, and the correct action was to decide now rather than to pre-register a sprint around it.",
    "Evidence that payment lapse is largely deliberate exit rather than involuntary failure — the 4,720 bucket then stops being the addressable prize, and neither a self-recovery rate nor a retry-timing fix answers the churn question the FY26 goal is set against.",
    "A cost per retained subscriber at day 90 for the 50% versus 30% first-box arms showing the offer, not the product, is driving the early-tenure loss — the whole intervention shortlist is then aimed at the wrong lever, and the sprint as scoped would not surface it."
  ],
  "what_to_test": [
    "Before committing the sprint: run the tenure-by-reason cross-tab cell counts only, as a same-day query on the existing export, and publish the counts. If cells are below interpretable size, the sprint's menu question is closed and the funding decision should be made immediately rather than in four weeks.",
    "Before committing the sprint: confirm with legal-privacy and analytics whether prior-cohort payment-lapse cancellations retain enough identifier after 180-day minimisation to detect unaided re-subscription, and over what window. Scope the self-recovery deliverable to the periods where the data still exists.",
    "Obtain the ALT-04 engineering cost and elapsed-time estimate before adopting it as a condition, not during the sprint window; the recommendation's own cost-per-retained-subscriber test cannot be applied to its own selected package without it.",
    "Compute cost per retained subscriber at day 90 for the 50% and 30% first-box arms from existing data as part of the same analyst window; it is the one number on the binding constraint that can be produced now and it is currently absent from the sprint scope.",
    "Test whether ALT-06 can be run concurrently rather than as a fallback: card-expiry reminders and retry re-sequencing sit inside the documented four-retry, no-retry-after-hard-decline envelope and need no new processor, so the cost of running them during the diagnostic window should be priced against a further quarter at 7.2% churn."
  ]
}

subscription_churn::baseline::v2.text:
{
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "statement": "Payment lapse is the single largest cancellation channel and its share has grown: 47.2% of 2026-Q2 cancellations (4,720) versus 40.9% in 2025-Q2.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-1f575ec3", "quote": "share_of_cancellations_percent: 47.2", "locator": "2026-Q2 payment_lapse row"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-1f575ec3", "quote": "cancellations: 4720", "locator": "2026-Q2 payment_lapse row"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-ec327dee", "quote": "share_of_cancellations_percent: 40.9", "locator": "2025-Q2 payment_lapse row"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Two dated rows from the same operational series, same dimension and buckets."
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "statement": "The payment-lapse channel is involuntary and closes automatically after four failed charge attempts, and no exit survey is offered on that path.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-1f575ec3", "quote": "involuntary - the subscription auto-closes after four failed charge attempts - synthetic", "locator": "note"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-1f575ec3", "quote": "exit_survey_offered: no", "locator": "2026-Q2 payment_lapse row"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Directly stated in the operational record."
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "statement": "The exit survey covers only the account-page cancellation flow, which was 34.1% of 2026-Q2 cancellations, and has no email, SMS or telephone equivalent — so the 47.2% payment-lapse group and the 18.7% support-contact group are never asked why they left.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-f6de8737", "quote": "share_of_cancellations_percent: 34.1", "locator": "2026-Q2 account_page row"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-f6de8737", "quote": "the exit survey is the final step of this flow - synthetic", "locator": "note"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-99848be3", "quote": "It has no email, SMS or telephone equivalent, and the research team cannot trigger it from any other system.", "locator": "Method"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-39dc52d8", "quote": "share_of_cancellations_percent: 18.7", "locator": "2026-Q2 support_contact row"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Channel shares from the operational series; coverage limitation stated by the research owner."
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "statement": "\"Recipe variety is the leading reason for churn\" is a finding about 89 self-selected respondents drawn from one cancellation channel, not about the cancelling population; responses are unweighted and completion is optional.",
      "claim_type": "assumption",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-c8392050", "quote": "response_count: 89", "locator": "recipe_variety row"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-99848be3", "quote": "214 free-text responses were collected between 2026-04-01 and 2026-06-30 and coded independently by two researchers into six reason categories.", "locator": "Method"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-99848be3", "quote": "The exit survey is presented as the final step of the account-page cancellation flow. Completion is optional and the subscriber can close the page without answering.", "locator": "Method"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-99848be3", "quote": "Responses are unweighted.", "locator": "Method"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "The denominator (214 optional responses from one of three channels) is stated in the source itself; generalising it to all cancellations is an inference the source does not license."
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "statement": "Churn is now concentrated in the first three weeks: 62.4% of 2026-Q2 cancellations occurred within 21 days, up from 48.1% in 2025-Q2, and median tenure at cancellation fell from 34 days to 19 days.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-cbdcb46d", "quote": "value: 62.4", "locator": "2026-Q2 share_of_cancellations_within_21_days"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-56ffaa2c", "quote": "value: 48.1", "locator": "2025-Q2 share_of_cancellations_within_21_days"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-6e95ea26", "quote": "value: 19", "locator": "2026-Q2 median_tenure_days_at_cancellation"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-176eac8a", "quote": "value: 34", "locator": "2025-Q2 median_tenure_days_at_cancellation"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Same metric, same source, two comparable periods."
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "statement": "New subscribers who cancelled within 7 days were far less likely to have chosen their box before the cutoff (38.2%) than subscribers retained at day 90 (81.7%), and were more likely to have reported a first-delivery issue (11.4% vs 4.3%).",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-420c010b", "quote": "value: 38.2", "locator": "menu_choice_made_before_cutoff, cancelled_within_7_days"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-38d31785", "quote": "value: 81.7", "locator": "menu_choice_made_before_cutoff, retained_at_day_90"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-8838d08d", "quote": "value: 11.4", "locator": "first_delivery_issue_reported, cancelled_within_7_days"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-bfe90214", "quote": "value: 4.3", "locator": "first_delivery_issue_reported, retained_at_day_90"}
      ],
      "support_level": "moderate",
      "rationale": "The measurements are solid but purely correlational; the direction of causation is untested and self-selection (people already intending to leave do not pick a box) is an equally consistent reading."
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "statement": "First boxes are operationally the weakest part of fulfilment: 71% of first boxes in 2026-Q2 were handled by Ashby, whose on-time rate was 88.7% against a network figure of 96.2% and a 95% standard, and substitutions ran at 9.6% on first boxes versus 3.9% on later boxes.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-74a9cee5", "quote": "New subscribers are routed to Ashby by default while the site builds volume, and Ashby handled 71% of first boxes in 2026-Q2.", "locator": "On-time delivery"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-74a9cee5", "quote": "By site: Rowan 97.4%, Pell 97.9%, Ashby 88.7%.", "locator": "On-time delivery"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-74a9cee5", "quote": "Network on-time delivery was 96.2% in 2026-Q2, comfortably above the 95% service standard.", "locator": "On-time delivery"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-74a9cee5", "quote": "Item substitutions were recorded on 9.6% of first boxes against 3.9% of subsequent boxes.", "locator": "Box accuracy"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Operations-owned report, dated 2026-07-22, with per-site figures."
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "statement": "Menu breadth cannot mechanically affect a subscriber's first two weeks, because weeks one and two are served from a fixed six-recipe starter menu outside the rotation.",
      "claim_type": "technical_constraint",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-74a9cee5", "quote": "Weeks one and two of a new subscription are served from a fixed starter menu of six best-selling recipes.", "locator": "Menu structure"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-74a9cee5", "quote": "A new subscriber sees their first rotation week in week three at the earliest.", "locator": "Menu structure"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Stated design of the product; relevant because 45.9% of cancellations occur in days 0-14."
    },
    {
      "id": "C9",
      "statement": "The expanded-menu pilot did not show a meaningful retention effect and by design says nothing about early-tenure subscribers.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-2b7ae6db", "quote": "Day-90 retention was 71.2% in the test arm against 70.4% in the control arm, with 1,900 subscribers per arm. The difference is not statistically meaningful.", "locator": "Expanded menu pilot"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-2b7ae6db", "quote": "The pilot excluded subscribers in their first 30 days by design, so as not to disturb the onboarding sequence. Nothing in the result speaks to early-tenure subscribers.", "locator": "Expanded menu pilot"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Randomised test, reported by research with its own scope limitation stated."
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "statement": "No test has ever been run on the dunning/card-update sequence, on any aspect of the first-box experience, or on whether menu breadth affects first-month subscribers.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-2b7ae6db", "quote": "No test has been run on the dunning or card-update sequence.", "locator": "Not attempted"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-2b7ae6db", "quote": "No test has been run on any aspect of the first-box experience.", "locator": "Not attempted"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-2b7ae6db", "quote": "No test has measured whether menu breadth affects subscribers in their first month.", "locator": "Not attempted"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Explicit statement of absence by the owning team."
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "statement": "The payment-failure path is not instrumented: the platform emits no cancellation event when a subscription closes on payment failure, so downstream systems including marketing receive nothing.",
      "claim_type": "technical_constraint",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-74a9cee5", "quote": "The subscription platform does not emit a cancellation event when a subscription is closed for payment failure. Downstream systems, including the marketing platform, receive nothing on that path.", "locator": "Data platform"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-2b7ae6db", "quote": "The marketing platform receives no cancellation event for subscriptions closed on payment failure, so that group could not be contacted.", "locator": "Win-back email test"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Corroborated by two independent sources (operations, research)."
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "statement": "Measurement of any change is delayed: menu selections and cancellation events reach the warehouse in a weekly batch and same-week measurement is not possible.",
      "claim_type": "technical_constraint",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-74a9cee5", "quote": "Menu selections and cancellation events reach the warehouse in a weekly batch. Same-week measurement of any change is not possible.", "locator": "Data platform"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Directly stated; sets a floor on experiment read-out cadence."
    },
    {
      "id": "C13",
      "statement": "Cost per retained subscriber is the binding constraint for FY26 and any proposal that raises it needs finance approval before a pilot may begin; the index has already moved from 97.5 in Q1 to 100.0 in Q2.",
      "claim_type": "business_constraint",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-07d95016", "quote": "Cost per retained subscriber is the binding constraint this year. Any proposal that raises it requires finance approval before a pilot may begin.", "locator": "Priorities and constraints"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-fd36f051", "quote": "value: 97.5", "locator": "2026-Q1 cost_per_retained_subscriber_index"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-66ac67b6", "quote": "value: 100.0", "locator": "2026-Q2 cost_per_retained_subscriber_index"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Policy statement plus two dated index rows; note the index is unitless and its construction is not documented in the evidence."
    },
    {
      "id": "C14",
      "statement": "Ashby cannot run more than 18 weekly recipes until a second pick line is commissioned in Q1 2027, so a return to a 20-recipe menu is not available at the site handling most first boxes within FY26.",
      "claim_type": "technical_constraint",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-74a9cee5", "quote": "Ashby cannot support more than 18 weekly recipes before a second pick line is commissioned. Commissioning is scheduled for Q1 2027.", "locator": "Capacity"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-3451c727", "quote": "**Decision:** reduce the weekly menu from 20 recipes to 14 from the week of 2025-10-06.", "locator": "OPS-2025-11"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Operations capacity statement with a dated commissioning plan."
    },
    {
